```

## Knowledge search (ksearch) — installed

```bash
python3 scripts/ksearch.py "<query>"          # excerpts + source lines; stdout capped at 2,400 UTF-8 bytes
python3 scripts/ksearch.py "<query>" --limit 3 --max-bytes 1600  # smaller result budget
python3 scripts/ksearch.py "<query>" --dir rules   # narrow to a subdir
python3 scripts/ksearch.py "<query>" --json   # machine-readable
python3 scripts/ksearch.py "<query>" --include-archive  # archived/artifact material too
```

Stdlib-only; reads `knowledge/` (override with `KB_ROOT`). The default limit is five results; `--max-bytes 0` opts into unbounded stdout. JSON stays an array and includes `line_start`, `line_end`, `heading`, and `truncated`. Budget omissions produce a short stderr notice; an unusably small budget exits 2. Bytes are not model tokens. Read cited lines when more evidence is needed.

Human-format searches are logged to `knowledge/_ksearch-log.tsv` for the zero-hit audit ([hygiene.md](../rules/hygiene.md)); JSON searches do not log. Set `KSEARCH_NO_LOG=1` to disable logging. See [retrieval evaluation](retrieval-evaluation.md) for project judgments and cost measurement.

```bash
python3 scripts/ksearch-eval.py knowledge/evals/cases.jsonl --root knowledge
python3 scripts/ksearch-eval.py knowledge/evals/cases.jsonl --root knowledge --max-bytes 1600 --json
```

## Code structure (ripgrep / ripwire) — recommended

```bash
rg "<literal>"                       # string literals, configs, non-code files
ripwire <dir> --for="<task>"         # ranked signatures for a task (if installed)
ripwire <dir> --callers=<SYM>        # who calls SYM
ripwire <dir> --impact=<SYM>         # transitive blast radius
ripwire <dir> --uses=<SYM>           # static read/write and reference sites
ripwire <dir> --exemplar="<helper>" # look for an existing implementation before adding one
ripwire <dir> --quality-delta       # inspect changed-code quality signals before completion
ripwire <dir> --test-gate           # static test-impact/coverage guidance; DOES NOT run tests
```

Prefer ripwire for structural discovery, impact, uses, and reuse candidates. It is optional: check `command -v ripwire` and `ripwire --help` for the installed capabilities; use targeted `rg` and source reads when unavailable. Static analysis can miss dynamic dispatch and generated code, so verify relevant source and execute the project's actual lint/typecheck/tests. `--test-gate` is guidance, not test execution or proof that tests pass. Cite source files rather than a generated map.

Choose the relevant source directory for `<dir>`. Init accepts repeated `--source-dir DIR` options; otherwise it detects conventional `src`, `lib`, `app`, or `frontend` directories, falling back to the repository root. Check these recorded choices against the task instead of treating them as an exhaustive code map.

## Skills

Canonical procedures live in [skills/](skills/README.md); [agent adapters](../agents/README.md) provide thin discovery stubs:

- **distill** — the bootstrap distillation pass (SCAFFOLDED → VERIFIED). Invoked via the stub, or by following [skills/distill.md](skills/distill.md) directly.
- **kb-audit** — the consolidation pass (query-log review, label spot-check, reference audit).
- **kb-handoff** — save or resume work in the active plan, including ownership and test evidence.
- **Optional workflow discovery (`--workflow` at init):** `kb-plan`, `kb-implement`, `kb-review`, `kb-board`. Their procedures remain readable even without discovery stubs.

## Knowledge gate (pre-commit)

```bash
python3 scripts/knowledge-gate.py          # staged changes; normal pre-commit use
python3 scripts/knowledge-gate.py --all    # full current working tree; useful in CI and audits
python3 scripts/kbase-doctor.py            # installation, integration, and KB diagnostics
python3 scripts/kbase-doctor.py --strict   # warnings also produce a nonzero exit
python3 scripts/kbase-doctor.py --json     # machine-readable diagnostics
python3 scripts/kbase-doctor.py --context-only --json  # local context sizes; no tool subprocesses
/path/to/kbase/bin/kbase doctor .          # same doctor from the scaffold checkout
```

The staged gate validates the commit's knowledge changes without consulting unrelated unstaged edits. The full-tree check validates the current checkout, including changes not staged. It does not replace the staged anti-truncation check. The installer creates `.pre-commit-config.yaml` or provides a merge snippet when a config already exists; activate the hook with `pre-commit install` after installing pre-commit through the project's tooling.

Doctor fails on structural errors. Unfilled project markers, an inactive hook, or unavailable optional ripwire produce warnings; use `--strict` when those should fail an onboarding or CI check. Its context inventory reports local instruction/import bytes, required INDEX reading, and skill metadata separately; character-based token estimates exclude unknown harness/global context and are not actual usage.

## Updating generated integrations

```bash
/path/to/kbase/bin/kbase update .          # preview proposed updates and conflicts
/path/to/kbase/bin/kbase update . --apply  # update eligible scripts and discovery stubs
```

`.kbase.json` records generated files and their installed content hashes. The updater applies only when every managed path is unchanged since installation or already matches the update; modified, deleted, or symlinked paths block application for review. It updates generated scripts and discovery stubs while preserving canonical knowledge, agent routers, and project policies. Use doctor after an update; a missing manifest requires reconciliation rather than a forced reinstall.
